  "description": "A parametric LIGHT BULB on the six-node spine — Geometry → Input Properties → Math Engine → Output Metrics → 2D Render + 3D Render.\n\nDROP IT ON A NODE. Pendant hangs it on a cord below the mount elevation, Ceiling and Wall fix it at the mount, and Table stands it the other way up — one variable, `_up`, carries that through every part.\n\nTHE ENVELOPE IS A REVOLVE, AND IT HAS TO BE. A Sphere row is DIMENSIONED: its radius lives in a field on the row, so nothing the Math Engine computes can drive it — and the attached-object runtime resolves only Polyhedron, Loft, Tube and Ring. A bulb driven by sliders therefore has to be `mlathe`, a profile swept about the lamp axis, which is parametric and renders identically in the flow overlay and once attached.\n\nTHE SILHOUETTE IS ONE FORMULA. The glass is `r = R·sin(theta)` swept from a starting angle to pi. That angle is not a guess: it is `asin(neckRadius / bulbRadius)`, so the bulb MEETS the neck at exactly the neck's radius and closes to a point at the tip. There is no asin builtin, so it is written as `atan2(x, sqrt(1 - x²))`, which is the same number. The t-coordinate is normalised by `(cos θ0 − cos θ) / (cos θ0 + 1)` so the sweep spans exactly the glass height whatever the angle worked out to.\n\nTHREE ENVELOPES, ONE BODY. Standard, Globe and Candle are not three shapes — they are three (radius, height) pairs fed to that one sweep. Everything downstream, including the profile, is unchanged.\n\nWHY THE PROFILE STARTS AT (0, 0). A lathe profile must close on the axis at both ends or the result is a surface, not a solid: the first point is on the axis at the cap end, the last is the tip. The neck is the two flat points between them.\n\nTHE CAP, CORD AND FILAMENT ARE TUBES along the same axis — a capped cylinder is exactly a Tube row, so none of them needs a mesh. The filament is five segments forming a supported loop, which is what makes the bulb read as a bulb at close range.\n\nDIMENSIONS AT THE DEFAULTS: a 60 mm envelope 76 mm tall over a 26 mm E26-scale cap, hanging 528 mm below a 2.70 m mount, lamp centre at 2.16 m.\n\nWHAT THIS CANNOT DO. The glass is opaque geometry — there is no transparency, no emission and no light cast; this is a fitting, not a luminaire. The cap is a plain cylinder with no thread, and there is no shade.",
